<?xml version="1.0"?>
<!--
  Cache budgets sized for the container this actually runs in.

  ClickHouse's defaults assume a large dedicated server: 8 GiB uncompressed cache, and
  5 GiB each for the mark, primary-index, index-mark and vector-similarity caches. That
  is 23+ GiB of permitted cache against a 6.6 GiB container whose max_server_memory_usage
  is 5.97 GiB, i.e. roughly four times the total memory the server is allowed to use.

  Caches count toward that total. So as a test run or a demo reads across many parts, the
  caches fill, tracked memory walks up to the cap, and OvercommitTracker then stops
  whichever query happens to be in flight. Measured: the killed queries were 20 to 60 MiB
  bystanders, while the largest real query in a full suite run was 239 MiB and the largest
  merge 5.89 MiB. Nothing that could account for a 5.97 GiB total on its own. That is what
  made the failure look random and move between tests, and it is why restarting appeared
  to fix it (empty caches) until they refilled.

  The budgets below leave roughly 4.5 GiB of the cap for queries and merges, an order of
  magnitude more headroom than this workload's measured peak.

  uncompressed_cache_size is 0 deliberately rather than merely reduced: reads consult it
  only when use_uncompressed_cache=1, which this project never sets, so on the default
  read path it is pure budget with no benefit.

  NOTE for editors: XML forbids a double hyphen inside a comment, so the prose here
  cannot use the double-hyphen dash the rest of this codebase does. A stray one makes
  ClickHouse refuse to start with "Failed to merge config".
-->
<clickhouse>
    <!--
      Total-memory allowance, raised deliberately and on evidence.

      ClickHouse's tracked total runs far above what the server actually holds. Sampled
      once a second across a full test run: tracked peaked at 5.77 GiB while resident
      memory at that same instant was 1.71 GiB, with a single query in flight, and the
      largest query anywhere in that window was 221 MiB (largest merge: 5.89 MiB).
      Nothing real accounted for the total. Resident memory never exceeded 1.96 GiB.

      The default cap is 0.9 * container RAM = 5.97 GiB, so that over-count reaches the
      cap and OvercommitTracker stops whichever query happens to be in flight. The
      victims were 20 to 60 MiB bystanders, which is why suite failures looked random
      and moved between tests.

      Raising the allowance to 12 GiB gives the over-count room without any real risk:
      measured resident usage is ~2 GiB against a 6.6 GiB container. The protection this
      would otherwise give up is restored properly below, as a PER-QUERY limit, so a
      genuinely runaway query fails by itself and says so instead of the server picking
      an unrelated victim.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage>12884901888</max_server_memory_usage>
    <!--
      The ratio must be raised alongside the absolute value. ClickHouse takes the MINIMUM
      of max_server_memory_usage and max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio * RAM, and this
      image pins the ratio at 0.9, so setting the absolute value alone is silently
      ineffective: system.server_settings still reported 5.97 GiB.
    -->
    <max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>2</max_server_memory_usage_to_ram_ratio>

    <mark_cache_size>536870912</mark_cache_size>
    <uncompressed_cache_size>0</uncompressed_cache_size>
    <primary_index_cache_size>268435456</primary_index_cache_size>
    <index_mark_cache_size>268435456</index_mark_cache_size>
    <index_uncompressed_cache_size>0</index_uncompressed_cache_size>
    <vector_similarity_index_cache_size>0</vector_similarity_index_cache_size>
</clickhouse>
